# Test modules that must not run beside anything else in their package.
#
# `scripts/test.sh` with LOOM_TEST_PARALLEL greater than 1 runs a package's
# modules concurrently on one emulator. EUnit gives concurrent tests no
# isolation: they share the node, so they share every VM-global resource.
# A module listed here is pulled out of the parallel group and run after it,
# alone, because something it reads or writes is global to the emulator and
# a concurrent sibling would change the answer.
#
# Format, one module per line:
#
#   <package>|<module>|<why this module cannot share the VM>
#
# The module is the EUnit module atom, which is the test file's path under
# `test/` with `/` replaced by `@` — `test/cap/mcp_test.gleam` is
# `cap@mcp_test`. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored.
#
# Rules the runner enforces:
#
#   - a declared module that does not exist in its package fails the run,
#     so a rename or a deletion cannot leave a stale line behind;
#   - an unknown package name, a missing reason, or a malformed line fails
#     the run.
#
# The reason is the point of the file. A module belongs here only when the
# resource it collides on can be named — an emulator-wide counter, a
# `persistent_term` slot, a singleton process. A test that merely fails
# sometimes under load does not belong here: that is a fixture with a
# wall-clock assumption in it, and parking it here would hide the bug and
# spend the parallel speedup on nothing.

# --- the VM-global atom counter --------------------------------------------
# `erlang:system_info(atom_count)` counts atoms for the whole node. These
# modules assert the count does not move across a measured window, which any
# concurrent test loading a module for the first time falsifies.
runtime|runtime@service_lifetime_test|asserts erlang:system_info(atom_count) is unchanged across a session lifecycle; the counter is node-wide
runtime|runtime@registry_test|asserts erlang:system_info(atom_count) is unchanged across strand address allocation and factory replacement; the counter is node-wide
client|client@serve_test|asserts erlang:system_info(atom_count) is unchanged across repeated instance assembly; the counter is node-wide

# --- the capability channel's persistent_term slot -------------------------
# `packages/cap/src/cap/internal/dispatch.gleam:16` installs the capability
# channel into one VM-global `persistent_term` slot, and `reset()` clears it.
# That single slot is deliberate — it is what stops a surviving process from
# picking up the next execution's token — so two tests installing a fake
# channel at once each see the other's, or none.
cap|cap_test|installs and resets the capability channel in cap/internal/dispatch's VM-global persistent_term slot
cap|cap@mcp_test|installs and resets the capability channel in cap/internal/dispatch's VM-global persistent_term slot
cap|runtime_test|installs and resets the capability channel in cap/internal/dispatch's VM-global persistent_term slot
cap|strand_test|depends on the capability channel in cap/internal/dispatch's VM-global persistent_term slot
ext|ext_test|start_serving resets cap/internal/dispatch's VM-global persistent_term slot for every serving test

# --- the node-wide httpc supervisor ----------------------------------------
# `restart_httpc_manager` and `restart_httpc_handler_supervisor` in
# `packages/provider/test/provider_http_test_ffi.erl` each `exit(Pid, kill)` a
# registered OTP process, because surviving that replacement is the property
# those tests state. `httpc_handler_sup` is registered once per node rather
# than once per profile — `httpc_sup:init/1` starts one beside the profile
# supervisor and `httpc_manager:do_start_handler/2` looks it up by name — so
# the kill takes every httpc handler in the emulator with it, whichever
# profile they serve. No profile arrangement makes that private.
provider|provider@http_test|kills the node-wide httpc_manager and httpc_handler_sup as the property under test, which severs every other httpc request in the emulator
